edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1061 8712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8ed3410-17c6-5b4e-801f-204a8559cabe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1061 8713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "143b8bf4-dc04-56fe-9f49-a538e4b75644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1061 8714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45c8bbe7-572c-5f45-8330-89214603ae4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1065 2165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\n \n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M,$ we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{3+7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1065 2347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1067 "The Midpoint Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497596 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497597 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497598 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497599 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497605 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497606 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497607 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497610 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497616 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497622 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497625 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497628 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497639 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186497644 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0f80bc47-7b56-5357-b4e7-718f4ddfa41b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497499 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497507 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497515 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497516 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497517 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497520 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497521 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497528 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497538 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497557 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497560 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497562 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497571 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1039 "Word Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186497578 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4154d81b-c94a-57f7-80fa-0daa341505e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0a5bdaa9-fe87-516a-8c94-9bc96565f389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1061 196434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "331a78d4-5c29-520f-a140-cfab1b9cf26b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1061 196435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bbbe689a-fd82-54c6-b18a-876c6124c4f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1061 196439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a86bd4f-c706-59ab-986e-311feb0009df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-4 \\\\ 6x-y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/59/59f16d57e74fd94fdab464eca22cea1271b19a3b78114446e31343cfabd15a12.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6).$\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations. \n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & \\,=\\,-4 \\\\ (2)-(6) & =-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & \\,=\\,6 \\\\ 6(2)-6 & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1065 8209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "84e2025e-9a3e-50ad-9b6b-dcc75fb2b7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176030 #edb/ref 